+
+=== section 6.1 example with a byte oriented MCU SPI port, DS21298E-page 21 (verbatim) ===
+rising edge. Because communication with the
+MCP3204/3208 devices may not need multiples of
+eight clocks, it will be necessary to provide more clocks
+than are required. This is usually done by sending
+‘leading zeros’ before the start bit. As an example,
+Figure 6-1 and Figure 6-2 illustrate how the MCP3204/
+3208 can be interfaced to a MCU with a hardware SPI
+port. Figure 6-1 depicts the operation shown in SPI
+Mode 0,0, which requires that the SCLK from the MCU
+idles in the ‘low’ state, while Figure 6-2 shows the
+similar case of SPI Mode 1,1, where the clock idles in
+the ‘high’ state. 
+As is shown in Figure 6-1, the first byte transmitted to
+the A/D converter contains five leading zeros before
+the start bit. Arranging the leading zeros this way
+allows the output 12 bits to fall in positions easily
+manipulated by the MCU. The MSB is clocked out of
+the A/D converter on the falling edge of clock number
+12. Once the second eight clocks have been sent to the
+device, the MCU’s receive buffer will contain three
+unknown bits (the output is at high impedance for the
+first two clocks), the null bit and the highest order four
+bits of the conversion. Once the third byte has been
+sent to the device, the receive register will contain the
+lowest order eight bits of the conversion results.
+Employing this method ensures simpler manipulation
+of the converted data.
+Figure 6-2 shows the same thing in SPI Mode 1,1,
+
+=== Figure 6-1 clock/bit alignment, DS21298E-page 21 (text extracted with coordinates) ===
+printed clock numbers 1..24 at x = 149.0 163.4 177.8 192.4 207.7 222.0 236.6 251.3 | 281.8 293.5 308.7 322.7 337.5 351.7 366.5 381.0 | 411.2 425.9 440.6 455.3 470.7 484.9 499.6 514.0
+MCU transmitted data row (first byte): 0 0 0 0 0 1 D2 D1 D0  -> five leading zeros then START at clock 6
+MCU received data row: byte 1 = 8 unknown bits (clocks 1..8); byte 2 = three unknown bits at x=285.1/299.9/314.8, (Null) at x=323.7, B11 at x=340.8, B10 at 355.1, B9 at 372.2, B8 at 386.9; byte 3 = B7..B0 at x=415.1 429.9 444.6 459.7 474.1 488.9 503.6 518.2
+=> with the start bit at clock 6, the null bit is latched at clock 12 (7th clock after the start) and B11..B0 at clocks 13..24 (8th..19th after the start).
+   The datasheet text states it directly: 'The MSB is clocked out of the A/D converter on the falling edge of clock number 12.'
+
+=== timing parameters, DS21298E-page 4/5, VDD = 5 V unless noted ===
+fCLK  max 2.0 MHz (5 V), 1.0 MHz (2.7 V); tHI/tLO >= 250 ns; tSUCS >= 100 ns;
+tSU >= 50 ns; tHD >= 50 ns; tDO <= 200 ns; tEN <= 200 ns; tDIS <= 100 ns; tCSH >= 500 ns.
+
+=== derived frame used by the review ===
+clock 1..5   DIN  START=1, SGL/DIFF=1, D2, D1, D0
+clock 6      DIN  don't care, sample/hold ends on its falling edge
+clock 7      DOUT null bit (low)
+clock 8..19  DOUT B11..B0, MSB first, latched by the master on rising edges
+total 19 SCLK pulses for one complete MSB-first conversion
